## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world streaming analytics: maintaining a dynamic feature (distinct count) under batched updates with tight per-batch guarantees. It probes data structures (circular buffers, counters), amortized reasoning, and practical optimizations under constrained space. 

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Distinct counting with frequency map
- Amortized analysis under batched updates
- Space-conscious data structures and optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation skeleton demonstrating the core idea
class WindowDistinct {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buf = new Array(W);
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.head = 0; // points to oldest element
    this.size = 0;
    this.distinct = 0;
  }
  init(arr) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.W; i++) this.push(arr[i]);
  }
  push(x) {
    if (this.size < this.W) this.size++;
    else {
      this.pop();
    }
    this.buf[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    const c = (this.freq.get(x) || 0) + 1;
    this.freq.set(x, c);
    if (c === 1) this.distinct++;
  }
  pop() {
    const idx = (this.head) % this.W;
    const y = this.buf[idx];
    const c = this.freq.get(y) - 1;
    if (c === 0) this.freq.delete(y);
    else this.freq.set(y, c);
    if (c === 0) this.distinct--;
  }
  batchUpdate(batch) {
    for (const x of batch) this.push(x);
  }
  query() {
    return this.distinct;
  }
}
```
